entry.description:
Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint. The primary source reports: . We consider the Robin Laplacian on a planar exterior domain with a negative boundary parameter. For a negative boundary parameter of a sufficiently large absolute value this operator has at least two negative eigenvalues below the bottom of the essential spectrum. We prove that under a fixed area constraint and convexity assumption on the bounded complement of the exterior domain the second Robin eigenvalue is maximized by the exterior of the disk. The inequality is strict for every exterior of a non-circular domain whenever the comparison disk has a negative second eigenvalue. The proof modifies the parallel-coordinate method by allowing the angular phase of a trial function to vary with the distance from the boundary. This result resolves the first item in [ KL24 , Conjecture 1.2] by the authors under an additional convexity assumption. Relaxing the convexity and the perimeter constraint remain open problems. Direct AI involvement: Primary paper disclosure (source-attributed excerpts): al profile as in [ KL24 ] , but uses a distance-dependent angular phase instead of distance-independent angular phase used in [ KL24 ] . This idea of distance-dependent angular phase was suggested by ChatGPT 6 Astra. To precisely formulate the main result of this paper, let us consider the closed, densely defined, symmetric and semi-bounded sesquilinear form (1.2) 𝔮 α Ω ext ​ [ u , v ] = ∫ Ω ext ∇ u ⋅ ∇ v ¯ ​ 𝑑 x + α ​ ∫ ∂ Ω u ​ v ¯ ​ 𝑑 s , dom ​ 𝔮 α Ω ext = H 1 ​ ( Ω ext ) , \mathfrak{q}_{\alpha}^{\Omega^{m ext}}[u,v]=\int_{\Omega^{{m ext}}}
